# Q: what happens where two crossings meet at one cell?
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --probe tools/probe/bridge-crossing.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/bridge-crossing.txt
#
# A cell can carry deck from two crossings at once, and the simulation allows it: the rule
# that a span runs the narrow way does not prevent two spans from crossing, and on this seed
# thousands of pairs of sites do. What that leaves open is whether the deck at the shared cell
# is one block or two drawn through each other, and it cannot be answered from the
# `bridges` list, because a junction is a fact about the cell rather than about either crossing.
#
# The pair used here is on the map's northern shore: a fifteen-cell span along row 0 from
# (-210, -295) m, and a span running south through column 22 from (-89, -295) m. They meet at
# cell (22,0), which is the only cell both lists contain.

focus -150 -290
zoom 260
pitch -0.95

# Both sites are crossings on their own, and they cost nothing a team at its start cannot pay.
bridge -210 -295
bridge -89.1 -295.3

# The first one, whole.
arm bridge
hover -210 -295
click
tick 500
bridges

# The second, across it. The placement is armed again because ordering one crossing ends the
# mode — a click that is accepted means the player is done, and the button has to be pressed
# again for the next one.
arm bridge
hover -89.1 -295.3
click
tick 200
bridges

# The shared cell: one block, running both ways, with both crossings through it.
block -89.1 -295.3
attributes -89.1 -295.3

hud off
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-crossing.png

# Close up on the junction, and from above it, where a crossroads deck is legible as one.
zoom 34
pitch -0.45
focus -89.1 -295.3 4
settle
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-junction-close.png

pitch -1.30
focus -89.1 -295.3 1
settle
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-junction-top.png

# And the whole crossroads from a distance, which is how a player meets it.
zoom 150
yaw 2.2
pitch -0.80
focus -110 -295.3
settle
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-crossing-wide.png
